WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:08.172
The world faces many challenges such as climate change, resource scarcity and inequality.

00:00:09.579 --> 00:00:15.993
To tackle these challenges, all UN member states adopted the 2030 Agenda in 2015, setting 17 

00:00:15.993 --> 00:00:21.470
Sustainable Development Goals, or SDGs, to be achieved by 2030. For engineers, they are 

00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:24.232
important because tell us which problems are worth solving.

00:00:24.232 --> 00:00:29.270
 One way they’re being addressed is with robots. But first, let us define what a robot is.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:34.999
The International Federation of Robotics, or IFR, describe a robot as “a&nbsp;programmed actuated 

00:00:34.999 --> 00:00:39.682
mechanism with a degree of autonomy to perform locomotion, manipulation&nbsp;or positioning”. 

00:00:39.682 --> 00:00:44.403
An industrial robot is an “automatically controlled, reprogrammable, multipurpose manipulator,

00:00:44.403 --> 00:00:49.105
 programmable in three or more axes, which can be either fixed in place or fixed to a mobile 

00:00:49.105 --> 00:00:53.155
platform for use in automation&nbsp;applications in an industrial environment”.

00:00:53.155 --> 00:00:57.174
 Finally, a service robot is a “robot&nbsp;in personal use or professional use 

00:00:57.174 --> 00:00:59.876
that performs useful tasks for humans or equipment”

00:01:00.853 --> 00:01:04.954
Industrial robots have typically been used for factory work such as welding and assembly

00:01:04.954 --> 00:01:09.286
 whereas service robots such as a robot vacuum work in non-factory consumer 

00:01:09.286 --> 00:01:14.298
and professional environments. However, both industrial and service robots are being used to 

00:01:14.298 --> 00:01:19.334
tackle the SDGs and the IFR describes some applications of these robots in their case studies.

00:01:20.094 --> 00:01:25.940
In Spain, a mobile robotic factory was trialled for solar panel assembly, supporting SDG 7. 

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:32.147
In Australia, collaborative robots are being developed for the automation of coral propagation

00:01:32.147 --> 00:01:36.809
 in an effort to restore coral reefs, supporting SDGs 13 and 14

00:01:38.157 --> 00:01:42.284
Researchers at the Woods Hole Oceanographic Institution have developed an autonomous 

00:01:42.284 --> 00:01:46.843
underwater robot that finds biodiversity hotspots in coral reefs which 

00:01:46.843 --> 00:01:51.540
was field tested in the US Virgin Islands. This supports SDG 14.

00:01:53.478 --> 00:01:56.706
This robot developed by Aerones, a company headquartered in the US 

00:01:56.706 --> 00:02:03.267
and Latvia, is used for repairing the leading edges of wind turbine blades, supporting SDGs 7 and 9

00:02:04.647 --> 00:02:08.588
In two German care homes, a social robot called Navel has been piloted 

00:02:08.588 --> 00:02:12.789
for social accompaniment and interaction with the residents, with the aim of supporting 

00:02:12.789 --> 00:02:15.767
their overall well-being which links to SDG 3.

00:02:16.325 --> 00:02:21.247
The bottom line is that robots are being applied across the SDGs, from clean energy to life below

00:02:21.247 --> 00:02:24.612
 water, through trials, pilots and research. The rest of this video focuses 

00:02:24.612 --> 00:02:28.721
on one SDG: Goal 3, good health and well-being.

00:02:28.969 --> 00:02:33.465
Within SDG 3, robots are being used to address many healthcare problems.

00:02:33.465 --> 00:02:38.287
 Complex surgery demands extreme precision, which is where surgical robots like the da Vinci

00:02:38.287 --> 00:02:43.454
 robot aim to help. They operate via small incisions in the body, providing steadier 

00:02:43.454 --> 00:02:48.000
instrument movement. They’ve been linked to faster recovery and shorter hospital stays.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:51.207
 Over 11,000 systems are installed worldwide.

00:02:52.091 --> 00:02:56.052
Hospital-acquired infections are another challenge: one modelling study estimated around

00:02:56.052 --> 00:03:03.000
 28,500 deaths in English NHS hospitals in 2016/17 attributed to them. Disinfection robots

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:07.744
 such as this robot from Akara Robotics provide a potential solution. Disinfection robots 

00:03:07.744 --> 00:03:12.306
autonomously navigate and decontaminate rooms using UV light to supplement manual cleaning. 

00:03:12.306 --> 00:03:16.720
Robots like this are being evaluated in the wards and operating theatres of Friarage Hospital

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:19.557
 in Northallerton, aiming to speed up room turnaround.

00:03:20.178 --> 00:03:24.515
Staff time is another challenge. In one observational study of a hospital emergency

00:03:24.515 --> 00:03:29.352
 department, nurses left patient rooms about eight times per eight-hour shift to find supplies,

00:03:29.352 --> 00:03:33.713
 spending about 16 minutes away from the bedside. Logistics robots such as this one from

00:03:33.713 --> 00:03:37.995
 Pudu Robotics can potentially take over these deliveries. They can deliver items such as

00:03:37.995 --> 00:03:40.792
 medications, instruments, meals and linens as well as navigate 

00:03:40.792 --> 00:03:44.765
narrow corridors and elevators with the aim to free clinical staff time 

00:03:45.559 --> 00:03:50.556
However, these robots also raise some challenges of their own. The first is access and cost.

00:03:50.556 --> 00:03:55.901
 Prices range from tens of thousands of dollars for delivery robots to millions for surgical 

00:03:55.901 --> 00:04:00.882
systems, and surgical robots in particular tend to be concentrated in wealthy countries like the US.

00:04:00.882 --> 00:04:05.562
 This uneven access could widen the gap SDG 3 aims to close.

00:04:06.917 --> 00:04:11.388
Another challenge is evidence and workflow issues. For example, the advantage of robotic

00:04:11.388 --> 00:04:16.706
 surgery over conventional keyhole surgery is incompletely proven; more research is needed on

00:04:16.706 --> 00:04:20.674
 that front. Training staff on new robots could also take time and disrupt

00:04:20.674 --> 00:04:23.357
 hospital workflows during the initial stages of adoption.

00:04:24.685 --> 00:04:30.339
The ethical considerations span accountability, trust, privacy, and biases and they affect robotics

00:04:30.339 --> 00:04:36.898
as a whole, not just medical robots. Liability rules are unclear if a robot harms a patient. Trust is

00:04:36.898 --> 00:04:41.814
partly shaped by science fiction and a desire for human contact in hospitals. Robots may store 

00:04:41.814 --> 00:04:44.934
sensitive patient data. And AI-driven robots trained on biased 

00:04:44.934 --> 00:04:48.411
datasets can amplify existing inequalities.

00:04:49.849 --> 00:04:55.567
To conclude, robots can help tackle SDG 3, but achieving good health for all requires lower-cost

00:04:55.567 --> 00:04:59.706
 robots alongside accountability and proven care.

